module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_hand_managed_proxy_edge_drops_a_route_its_consumers_read

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

// THE CLASS: a proxy that stands between a reader and a service is hand-managed, its forwarded-route set
// is never re-derived from the readers who consume the routes, and it silently withholds one of them. The
// reader gets a 404 (or a connection error) on a route the SERVICE does answer, and the omission is legal
// from the proxy's own point of view -- a proxy that forwards a subset is a well-formed proxy. Only the
// consumer knows a route is missing, and no row joined the proxy's forwarded set to that consumer, so
// nothing could say "this front does not render." This is the gunbc.design_argument::consumption failure
// in wire form: an un-consumed requirement is invisible to the producer, and a hand-written producer is
// the producer that never re-derives. Repair at the EARLIEST boundary (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary):
// the forwarded-route SET itself, which had no producing authority -- make it a consumed, derived set so
// the omission is unrenderable (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder, gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed).

data a_hand_managed_proxy_edge_drops_a_route_its_consumers_read: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_hand_managed_proxy_edge_drops_a_route_its_consumers_read" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the live-deploy leg that emits a serving engine's TLS front renders its server block from gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_proxy_config -- which takes no forwarded-route list and builds the server from gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_required_routes, so a front that omits a consumed route has no constructor -- and the hand-managed /opt/gunbc-serving/*/nginx.conf is retired. That emitter now EXISTS as gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_render (general over any engine front), which srv4 instantiates as data via gunbc.serving.srv4_daily_engine_front::srv4_daily_engine_front_inputs; it renders the front through engine_front_proxy_config and forwards /metrics by roster; it is still a refused frontier, not a deployer: srv4's own front parameters (bind/listen/cert/key/loopback host+port) are typed Unread (none is modeled; srv4_daily_engine_front_inputs), so the render refuses until they are read, and the live hand-managed file is still the only thing on the host. The climb past Mitigatable needs three things: (1) read srv4's six front parameters and re-realize srv4_daily_engine_front_inputs, (2) capture the host file's sha256sum as a host-fact read (Part A recorded none), then (3) the live-deploy leg deploys the rendered bytes and retires the hand-managed file. Until all three land, the live state is detected (engine_front_unserved_consumers names the blocked consumer) but not prevented -- hence Mitigatable, not higher" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a TLS front door in front of a service forwards a SUBSET of the routes the service answers, and the omitted route is one a fleet reader consumes. The proxy is well-formed (forwarding a subset is legal), the reader gets 404 and blames the service or the network, and no row in the model can say the front is wrong because no row produced the front. Nothing refuses it and nothing renders it -- the omitted route was never a consumed requirement, so the producer never re-derived it.**",

    "FOUND: 2026-11-24, srv4 daily engine (the Strata gunbc-srv4-daily-engine behind /opt/gunbc-serving/srv4-daily/nginx.conf), while the operator's session measured the fleet tuning. That front forwards /v1/ and answers `location / { return 404; }`, so its /metrics is unreachable by name from any fleet reader. PROVENANCE, stated because the tree does not hold it and this PR does not touch the host: this is an operator-observed receipt of the 2026-11-24 session (extdeps.external_authority::CitedFigureStanding), not a read the repo's own instruments produced (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility). The engine answers /metrics on its own port; only the front withholds it.",

    "WHICH ROW SHOULD HAVE RENDERED THE FRONT, AND DID NOT. The three nearest candidates are each a different proxy with a different contract, which is why none of them is this row: gunbc.serving.router_proxy_config renders the ROUTER's byte door (the authz callout on srv1) and dissolves with that proxy when the v2-native door carries bytes; gunbc.serving.serving_front_door is the APPLICATION seat-permit door (the HMAC grant that lets a caller forward one request), not the nginx front in front of one engine; and no srv4 daily-engine module renders a front at all. The per-engine TLS front door had NO producing row -- that absence is the earliest unjustified boundary, and the fix is to name it (gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_proxy_config) and derive its forwarded-route set from the consumers (gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_required_routes).",

    "WHO CONSUMES /metrics (the consumption answer the boundary needs). NOT the router -- the router is one of the readers that cannot reach srv4's /metrics today. The named consumers are the availability and progress readers and the memory-provenance readers: the model+metrics probe gunbc.serving.serving_availability::serving_availability_bracketing_read, the two-/metrics-body stall reading gunbc.serving.engine_progress::engine_progress_read, and the /metrics provenance reads carried as cited figures in gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::srv4_serving_engine_memory (expert_cache_mib, conversation_cache_mib, conversation_cache_min_free_mib). The required route is a row only because these readers read it (gunbc.design_argument::consumption).",

    "THE OTHER TWO OBSERVABILITY GAPS OF THE SAME DAY, AND WHERE THEIR STANDING BELONGS. (1) The per-cause cold-token breakdown (same-card intact / same-card rewritten / rebound / no-probe) that carried the day was produced by ad-hoc scripts (/tmp/rlog.py over the router's prefixprobe/usageprobe/upgrade-move lines; /tmp/snap.py and /tmp/delta.py differencing engine /metrics; the Strata per-request reuse line). Its honest standing: it SHOULD become a named instrument, but NOT YET -- a declared frontier with a named trigger, not a refusal (gunbc.design_argument::consumption -- consumed by a named later change, with its trigger). Its CONSUMER is gunbc.plans.fabric_serving_scheduler (PR #17076, pending operator merge) done-when 4, the resident-prefix-vs-fairness decision that prices the ~0% GPU-resident-hit / ~112k-token reload regime; its DISCRIMINATOR is a turn's reused tokens vs its prompt tokens, per cause. It is not built now only because its PRODUCER does not exist: the inputs are the hand-managed router's TEMPORARY prefixprobe/usageprobe lines (gap (2) below), which gunbc.serving.router_realization does not emit. It becomes a row in gunbc.instrument_targets when class B5's modelled router emits prefix-overlap / token-usage observations -- which is what ties the two gaps together. An instrument_targets row is a `gunbc test <label>` run on dispatch (gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow), NOT a rostered CI job, so the job-roster and CI-budget closures do NOT bear on it and are not reasons here; the only admission rule is consumed from its owner, gunbc.design_document::ci_contract_blocks (a new wide instrument must first name its real consumer, discriminator and acceptable cost -- all three named above; only the producer is absent), and no gate is built here ahead of the producer (gunbc.design_ruling::bad_process_is_worse_than_no_process argues against keeping a local copy of that rule). The measured per-cause counts stand as an operator-observed receipt of that session (extdeps.external_authority::CitedFigureStanding), not deduced facts. (2) The router's prefixprobe/usageprobe lines are marked TEMPORARY (2026-10-27/28, calm-crab-645) in the HAND-MANAGED srv1 ~/router/nginx.conf, so their standing is a transient receipt of the hand-managed router, NOT a fact of the modelled router -- gunbc.serving.router_realization emits no such log line. Their standing belongs HERE, in this row (the owning model), pointed at the modelled replacement rows that will retire them -- gunbc.serving.router_realization::router_realization_contract, gunbc.serving.turn_admission::serving_co_tenancy, gunbc.serving.serving_relocation::admit_state_method (class B5) -- which must first carry the same prefix-overlap and token-usage facts as typed observations on the authority that decides the move. They are deliberately not deleted while the router stays hand-managed. No standing carrier is kept in gunbc.serving.serving_observability: the only fold that could read one lives in class B5's router realization (a fork B5 owns), and a record no fold reads is dangling (gunbc.design_argument::consumption), so the three retiring rows are cited as evidence below instead.",

    "RUNG ADVANCE (2026-11-25, stacked on this class): srv4's front is now RENDERABLE FROM the roster -- the general gunbc.serving.serving_observability::engine_front_render renders srv4's front data (srv4_daily_engine_front_inputs) through engine_front_proxy_config (no second nginx emitter; the bytes come from extdeps.proxy.openresty via that one builder), so the rendered front always forwards /metrics. The render is a refused frontier, not yet a deploy: srv4's own front parameters are typed Unread (none modeled; the router's tailnet upstream in gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy is a different fact from the front's loopback target and is not reused), so engine_front_render REFUSES until they are read and engine_front_rendered_sha is Absent while refused -- it never renders placeholder bytes or a sha of a placeholder (parent ruling 2026-11-25). gunbc.serving.srv4_daily_engine_front::srv4_engine_front_divergence records the disagreement with the live host: the model render forwards /metrics, the host omitted it (the transcribed 2026-11-24 content fact above), and BOTH shas are pending -- host_file_sha is the Unread FrontParam<Sha256Digest> (a host-fact sha256sum read is owed; Part A captured none) and rendered_sha is Absent (blocked behind the unread parameters). The host is NOT touched by this PR.",

    "CEILING AND ITS TELL: StructurallyGuaranteed, because the front is RENDERED FROM the roster (engine_front_proxy_config takes no forwarded-route list), so once the live-deploy emitter consumes it a front that omits a consumed route has no constructor at all -- not refused, unauthorable. The current rung is Mitigatable: the model DETECTS the misconfiguration -- engine_front_unserved_consumers, reconciling the host's OBSERVED forwarded set against the roster, names the consumer srv4 shut out -- but nothing on the required path prevents the live hand-managed front until the emitter lands, so the rung is detection, not prevention. The tell that the ceiling has NOT been reached: a serving engine whose /metrics is unreachable by name from a fleet reader while the engine answers it on its own port, which is the srv4 state this row was found at.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_observability", decl_name: "engine_front_proxy_config", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_observability", decl_name: "engine_front_unserved_consumers", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_observability", decl_name: "engine_front_required_routes", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_observability", decl_name: "engine_front_render", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_observability", decl_name: "engine_front_rendered_sha", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv4_daily_engine_front", decl_name: "srv4_engine_front_divergence", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.router_realization", decl_name: "router_realization_contract", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.turn_admission", decl_name: "serving_co_tenancy", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_relocation", decl_name: "admit_state_method", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_availability", decl_name: "serving_availability_bracketing_read", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.engine_progress", decl_name: "engine_progress_read", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv4_serving_engine_memory", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "bad_process_is_worse_than_no_process", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ]
}
